A||||5||Homicidal||Ummmmmmmmmmmmm.....did I burn the amp?????? ||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-13-2000||12:50 AM||||I was testing the power of the amp and I hooked the B+ (battery) wire to the positive and the ground to the<BR>                          negative and I don't think anything happened. So I did the same thing, but hooked the battery to the negative and<BR>                          ground to the positive. I saw a spark. Did I do something to hurt the amp? <P>                          I checked the fuse in the amp and it's fine. <BR>                          I was a dumb@ss not to realize I needed my remote turn on wire for it to show power. But now I know. <P>                          But is there a chance I fried the amp? Hope not! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||04-13-2000||01:49 AM||||What kind of amp is it? Does the amp have a funny smell now?  And how long did you keep it hooked up the wrong way?||||reg||5
Z||000002||batboy||04-13-2000||01:55 AM||||Probably ok, it's typical to get some sparks when messing around the battery. Probably better not do that anymore though. The main power wire is basically always hot. The remote wire connects to a switched power supply, so when you turn on the ignition the remote turns hot which activates the main internal switch in the amp which powers it up. As long as the remote wire wasn't powered, you shouldn't of got any juice into the amp all.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Homicidal||04-13-2000||02:07 AM||||I tried it again though, this time with the RMT also connected to the battery + and I saw some sparks again, this time I held the power wire and RMT wire together while my other hand held the ground to metal and then I touched the 2 wires to the + terminal.  Then I small a tiny spark.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Haze_Performance||04-13-2000||02:28 AM||||Anytime you hook up power up to an amp you will see a tiny spark. When hooking up the amp you should hook up the ground wire first then +.(as told to me by my local shop)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiasco||04-13-2000||03:55 AM||||I don't think you're gonna get anything BUT a spark unless the remote lead is connected to something appropriate like the blue remote lead from a head unit or a power- antenna/accessory wire or terminal. I'm pretty sure the RMT terminal on the amp needs a low power (accessory, etc) signal. -It's not just another 12V power lead.||||reg||1
